   145	Ordinary structured mutation re-serializes the whole diagram canonically.
   146	Source-preserving mode instead returns the authored source with only the
   147	edited statements changed. It accepts every operation of every structured
   148	family:
   149	
   150	1. The operations run as an ordinary structured edit; an operation that fails
   151	   there fails here with the same error and index.
   152	2. The difference between the canonical text before and after the edit is
   153	   replayed on the authored lines. Each canonical statement is traced to the
   154	   authored line that produced it (by the family source map's identities, by
   155	   identical spelling, or by that line's own parsed reading). A changed value is
   156	   spliced into the line that spells it, keeping its quoting; a line whose
   157	   every statement goes away is deleted; a new statement is written in
   158	   canonical spelling beside its neighbour, indented like its authored
   159	   siblings; a reordered statement keeps its authored line. Comments, blank
   160	   lines, directives, frontmatter, `:::class` shorthand and every other line
   161	   are copied byte for byte.
   162	3. The candidate is published only when the shared parser reads it back as the
   163	   same diagram as the canonical edit: the same facts and the same canonical
   164	   statements. Statement order the facts do not carry (declaration order, for
   165	   example) may follow the authored order. It must also leave no statement
   166	   before the entity it applies to that the authored source did not have
   167	   there: Mermaid 12.2.0 applies a flowchart `class` or `click`, and a Class
   168	   `style`, `cssClass`, link or `classDef`, only to an entity (or style class
   169	   membership) that exists where the statement stands, though our reader
   170	   applies it anyway (the family's `existingTargets`, which `verify` also
   171	   reports).
   172	4. When no local change passes, the canonical source is returned with
   173	   `preservation: { method: 'canonical', reason }`; otherwise the result
   174	   carries `preservation: { method: 'local' }`. A diagram that was not parsed
   175	   from source has no authored lines and is always written canonically.
   176	
   177	New statements are written in the same portable spelling as canonical
   178	serialization and reference existing elements by their identities. A
   179	statement whose authored line also carries other statements (a node declared
   180	inline on an edge) is edited inside that line where the changed text is found
   181	once; otherwise the line is rewritten as its remaining statements. A
   257	that would have to follow `S --> T` past a note that declares `T` there; the
   258	receipt names the facts the closest candidate reads otherwise), a classDef
   259	that would have to pass another classDef or a `style` to follow the new
   260	`cssClass` it needs (the receipt names the classDef and the class whose
   261	style-class assignment it would precede), an
   262	edit that empties a family below its grammar floor, a canonical difference
   263	beyond the diff budget, and edits to wrappers the canonical writer does not
   264	own. The receipt names the reason (the statement that could not be traced or
   265	placed, or how the closest local candidate reads differently from the edit);
   266	the source is then the ordinary canonical result.
   267	
   268	The same explicit mode, for structured and opaque bodies, is available
   269	through the workflow surfaces:
   270	
   271	- Library batches: `applyOps({ source, ops, mode: 'source-preserving' })`.
   272	- CLI: `am mutate diagram.mmd --op '<JSON>' --preserve-source`; JSONL mutation
   273	  requests use `preserveSource: true`.
   274	- Declarative MCP `mutate`: pass `mode: 'source-preserving'` with `source` and
   275	  `ops`.
   276	- Code Mode: `mermaid.mutatePreservingSource(diagram, op)` returns the same
   277	  `{ source, diagram }` result as the library. Returning that successful result
   278	  or its diagram produces a verified envelope with the exact edited source.
   279	- Editor adapter: programmatic `applyOps(source, ops, 'source-preserving')`
   280	  queues the edit in the persistent worker; it does not add an editor control.
   281	
   282	Batch success includes `mode: 'source-preserving'`, verification diagnostics
   283	and, for a structured body, the `preservation` receipt. A structured batch is
   284	projected as one edit, then op by op when that does not read back. A failed
   285	batch emits no edited source, even when earlier operations succeeded. Builders
   286	have no original source and do not accept this mode. On opaque bodies these
   287	entrypoints do not remove the portability warning or promise portable Mermaid
   288	export.
   289	
   290	Structured mode remains the default in 0.5.x so scripts that compare `am mutate`
   291	output with `am format` keep working; 0.6.0 makes source-preserving the default
   292	for `am mutate` and MCP `mutate`. Pin today's output with `--canonical` or
   293	`mode: 'structured'`.
   294	
   295	## Why not every syntax construct?
   265	 * Code Mode facade, and the CLI `--op`/`--ops` path. Typed callers keep using
   266	 * `mutate` directly, where the compiler already guarantees op shape;
   267	 * `mutateChecked` restores that guarantee at the boundaries where ops arrive as
   268	 * raw JSON.
   269	 *
   270	 * `validateOp` proves the op's SHAPE (field names, primitive types, enums)
   271	 * BEFORE the mutator runs, so a malformed op is rejected with a prescriptive
   272	 * INVALID_OP error instead of silently mangling the diagram. Semantics — does
   273	 * the referenced node exist, is the id a duplicate — stay in the mutator, which
   274	 * runs second on a shape-proven op. Result shape is identical to `mutate`, so
   275	 * this is a drop-in wherever the raw mutator was called with untyped ops.
   276	 */
   277	export function mutateChecked<D extends ParsedDiagram>(d: D, op: unknown): Result<D, MutationError>
