     2	// Source projection: a typed edit to a structured body, written as the
     3	// smallest change to the source it was read from.
     4	//
     5	// The canonical writer is a function of the typed model, so the line
     6	// difference between canonical(before) and canonical(after) is exactly what
     7	// the edit changed. Each canonical statement of `before` is traced to the
     8	// authored line that produced it: the line the family source map locates for
     9	// the same identity, else a line already in canonical spelling, else the line
    10	// whose isolated reading (the family parser and writer on that line alone)
    11	// contains it; respelled block contents pair by order. The difference is then
    12	// replayed on the authored lines: a changed token is spliced into the line
    13	// that spells it (through the line's canonical reading when the line keeps
    14	// its authored spacing), a line whose every statement goes away is deleted
    15	// (with any declaration folded into them), and a new statement is written in
    16	// canonical, portable spelling beside its neighbour, indented like its
    17	// authored siblings. Every other line is copied byte for byte.
    18	//
    19	// The projection only proposes. A few placement variants are tried, those
    20	// that rewrite the fewest authored lines first (ties in a fixed order), and
    21	// a candidate is returned only when the shared parser reads it back as the
    22	// same diagram as the canonical result: the same facts and the same canonical
    23	// statements (whose incidental order may follow the authored order). When no
    24	// candidate does, a second pass traces statements written over several
    25	// physical lines as one authored line, and traces a statement the parser
    26	// merges from several declarations (a label given again, a class named by
    27	// each `Class : member` line, a block opened twice) to every line it is read
    28	// from: a change is spliced into each line that spells it, a removal takes
    29	// every line that declares nothing else, and a new member of a block written
    30	// as separate lines is spelled like its authored siblings.
    31	//
    32	// A third pass reads the authored block structure: a block member is traced
    33	// to the line inside its authored block that declares it, a new statement
    34	// goes outside authored blocks its canonical statement is not in, and the
    35	// statements a rewritten line keeps and those written beside it are written
    36	// in canonical order. A declaration whose label the writer moves onto a new
    37	// statement keeps its line, and the new statement names it bare. A brace
    38	// opener the parser does not accept empty (a namespace) is read as a block,
    39	// and a block written on one line is rewritten there as the edited block.
    40	//
    41	// A candidate that reads back as the edit is still rejected when it leaves a
    42	// statement the family applies only to an entity that already exists (a
    43	// flowchart `class B hot` or `click B`, a Class `style B`) before that
    44	// entity's first mention, where the authored source did not: our reader
    45	// applies it, Mermaid ignores it (FamilyOperations.existingTargets).
    46	//
    47	// A last pass keeps each kept line after the first mention it was written
    48	// after. When a closer candidate misreads a name, or leaves such a statement
    49	// before it, and the edit removed every earlier line naming it, the statement
    50	// on the first kept line naming it (`note right of S` once `[*] --> S` goes,
    51	// which would declare S where it stands) moves verbatim to just after the
    52	// next line naming it, with the kept lines between that apply to it only
    53	// where it exists. A line read as nothing alone (a flowchart `click B`) is
    54	// traced by its reading after the earlier lines that share a word with it.
    55	//
    56	// A placement pass then writes a new statement such a candidate leaves
    57	// before what it needs (`class D hot` beside a classDef above D, a Class
    58	// classDef before the `cssClass` giving a class its style class) after it,
    59	// and a kept classDef a new `cssClass` would leave before its class after
    60	// that `cssClass`, unless the `cssClass` can stand between them; no move
    61	// passes a statement sharing a word with the one moved.
    62	//
    63	// Every candidate is also checked for a statement whose paint Mermaid draws
    64	// over where ours does not (FamilyOperations.existingTargets' `follows`): a
    65	// Class `style B` left before the classDef of B's style class setting the
    66	// same property, which Mermaid draws in statement order. A candidate that
    67	// leaves one the authored source does not have, however it moved, kept or
    68	// rewrote its lines, is rejected; the placement pass then writes the new or
    69	// edited one of the two on the other side of the kept one.
    70	// The caller otherwise writes the canonical source and says why: the closest
    71	// candidate's misreading, or the statement that could not be placed.
    72	// ============================================================================
